    [
     1,
     "Hi all,<p>I used probe2wayRC function to probe one of the significant latent interaction in my model. Here is the auction I used: result2wayRC &lt;- probe2WayRC(fitRC2way, c(&quot;f1&quot;, &quot;f2&quot;, &quot;f12&quot;), &quot;f3&quot;, &quot;f2&quot;, c(-1, 0, 1)). <br>    result2wayRC<p>I want to ask that, in this function, values of -1, 0, and 1 (i.e., c(-1, 0, 1)) stands for 1 sd below the mean, mean, and 1 sd above the mean of latent moderating factor?  </p></p>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "umXHFDCJCgAJ",
   "from": "Alex Schoemann",
   "author": "5d4f9e7f97c21008",
   "date": "2018-12-22T15:21:01Z",
   "subject": "Re: probe2wayRC function",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Yes, the -1, 0 and 1 are the values at which the simple slopes will be estimated. You don&#39;t have to use -1, 0, and 1 but they are the defaults. If you&#39;re using a fixed factor methods of scale setting (fixing latent variances to 1 and latent means to 0) then this provides estimates at 1 SD before the mean, the mean, and 1 SD above the mean for the moderator. If you&#39;re using another method of scale setting, it is important to examine simple slopes at meaningful values for the moderator.<div><br></div><div>Alex</div></div>"
    ],
    [
